</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hoang N. Phan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id8" class="ltx_text">Nanyang Technological University
</span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><span id="id10" class="ltx_text"> {cuong.le, tien.n.nguyen}@utdallas.edu
</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tien N. Nguyen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id11" class="ltx_text">The University of Texas at Dallas</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Specifications are vital for ensuring program correctness, yet writing them manually remains challenging and time-intensive.
Recent large language model (LLM)-based methods have shown successes in generating specifications such as postconditions, but existing single-pass prompting often yields inaccurate results. In this paper, we present <span id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span>, a novel framework for postcondition generation that treats LLMs as interactive and exploratory reasoners rather than one-shot generators. <span id="abstract1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span> employs feedback-driven multi-turn prompting approaches, enabling the model to iteratively refine candidate postconditions by incorporating implicit and explicit correctness feedback, while autonomously deciding when to stop. This process fosters deeper code comprehension and improves alignment with true program behavior via exploratory attempts. Our empirical evaluation shows that <span id="abstract1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span> significantly outperforms state-of-the-art approaches in both accuracy and completeness of generated postconditions.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Program specifications (pre-/postconditions) are central to checking that program behavior matches intent, but writing them manually is tedious. Prior work on automated inference broadly falls into four categories. <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">First</span>, program-analysis methods infer invariants dynamically from executions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beschastnikh et al. (2011)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ernst et al. (1999)</a></cite>—and thus are coverage-limited—or statically from code, often at the cost of conservative, false-positive–prone results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramanathan et al. (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2011)</a></cite>. <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Second</span>, data-mining approaches extract common API-usage patterns (e.g., call pairs/sequences and automata) from large codebases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wasylkowski et al. (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thummalapenta and Xie (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradel and Gross (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wasylkowski and Zeller (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2009a)</a></cite>, but typically do not infer semantic specifications such as pre-/postconditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Third</span>, EvoSpex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Molina et al. (2021)</a></cite> uses execution-guided evolutionary search to evolve postcondition candidates, yet its handcrafted operators
only weakly exploit program semantics, leading to incomplete or brittle postconditions. <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Fourth</span>, <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite> leverages LLMs to translate code and informal documentation into postconditions. The limitation of simple prompting in <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> lies in their reliance on <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-pass generation</span>: <span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> expects the LLM to generate a correct and complete postcondition in a single prompt.
Such prompting results in <span id="S1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">incorrect postconditions</span> that are syntactically plausible but semantically inaccurate, failing correctness tests or lacking discriminative power for bug detection.
(Related work details are given in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Related Work ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20610v1/fig1_cropped.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/214;" width="381" height="214" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Example of task #69 from EvalPlus with postconditions from Single-pass (nl2postcond <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite>), Greedy Multi-turn, and Exploratory Multi-turn. Blue blocks show model reasoning (omitted for all but turn 5 of Exploratory Multi-turn for space reason), red, orange, and gray blocks show submission and exploration attempts, and feedback. <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span>: correct and complete postcondition, <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>: otherwise.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our work, <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span>, we aim to enhance the LLM’s capability in generating postconditions from a given source code. A more robust alternative is <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">retry-based prompting</span> (<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Greedy Multi-turn</span> or Greedy for short), where the LLM is iteratively asked to regenerate postconditions until one passes a soundness check and exceeds a certain threshold of completeness. While this approach introduces feedback into the loop, it remains <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reactive and externally controlled</span> – the LLM is not actively involved in the reasoning process but simply retries in response to failure signals.
Thus, we also propose <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploratory Multi-Turn</span> (Exploratory for short), offering a more cognitively aligned framework in which the LLM is treated not just as a <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">code-to-code generator</span>, but as an <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">interactive reasoner</span>. We prompt the LLM to autonomously perform the exploration, internalize the reasoning process to derive the postcondition, and decide by itself when to stop the process. <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We prompt the model to perform one or multiple turns for exploration</span>, i.e., submitting postconditions to obtain the intermediate feedback on soundness and completeness, before deciding to submit the final postcondition. <span id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This method engages the model in an iterative self-refinement process where it internalizes the feedback, reflects on prior attempts with the observations on the results of the intermediate turns, and gradually converges toward a more accurate and comprehensive postcondition</span>. This feedback to LLMs can be implicit (e.g., soundness and/or completeness signals) or explicit (e.g., un-caught mutants). Greedy is a special case of Exploratory Multi-turn where no intermediate turn of exploration is allowed and the model submits the final result after each attempt. The key benefits of Exploratory Multi-turn include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deeper Code Comprehension</span>: By interacting with feedback and refining its outputs over multiple turns, the model is encouraged to form a more semantically grounded understanding of the code logic, rather than relying on superficial patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Autonomous Reasoning</span>: In the autonomous exploration, the model determines when to stop, which introduces a degree of self-awareness and decision-making on the sufficiency of its answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated our model on EvalPlus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite> and FixEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anjum Haque et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Exploratory Multi-turn achieves 99.4% correctness and 89.6% completeness, relatively improving over the baseline <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> by 26.1% in correctness and 2.48X in completeness. The average increase of completeness after one submission attempt of Exploratory Multi-turn is 1.67X higher than the Greedy’s. <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The postconditions from our tool can detect 1.42X-2.14X more bugs than <span id="S1.p6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> in FixEval with less costs (See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Bug Detection via Generated Postconditions ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>)</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Illustration</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The function <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span> in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">takes a non-empty list of positive integers as input and returns the greatest integer in the list whose frequency is greater than or equal to its value; if no such integer exists, it returns <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. In the ground truth, the complete specification for this function must contain all of the following sub-conditions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1) the return value is either <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> or a positive integer from the input list;</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2) if the return value is positive, its frequency must be greater than or equal to its value;</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(3) if the return value is positive, it must be the greatest integer satisfying the frequency condition;</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(4) if the return value is <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, no integer in the list satisfies the frequency condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We applied the state-of-the-art, LLM-based, postcondition generation tool, <span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite> on the code of the <span id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span> function.
<span id="S2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span> leverages a <span id="S2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-pass</span> prompting strategy to request Llama 4 Scout to generate the postcondition for the given code.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A candidate postcondition can be evaluated using two metrics: <span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correctness and completeness</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite>. <span id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness</span> refers to if the postcondition passes all test cases in the given test set. A postcondition is correct if it holds true for all test cases that the original function passes (no contradiction to the behavior in test cases). However, correctness alone is not sufficient as a trivial postcondition like <span id="S2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"True"</span> is always correct but useless in bug detection. <span id="S2.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness</span>, referring to discriminative power, measures how informative the postcondition is. It reflects the postcondition’s ability to detect incorrect versions of the function (e.g., mutated code). This is done by checking if the postcondition can "catch" mutants (i.e., detecting bugs), i.e., the modified versions that behave differently.
Formally, completeness is the percentage of mutants that can be distinguished (i.e., rejected) by the postcondition when used with the test suite. We made the following observations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">1. The state-of-the-art LLM-based, <span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Single-pass</span><span id="S2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> approach, </span><span id="S2.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S2.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite>,
generated the postconditions ensuring that the return value is either -1 or a positive integer from the input list, thereby satisfying the condition (1). However, none of the conditions (2)–(4) was generated.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. <span id="S2.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Feedback-driven Multi-Turn Approaches</span>. Prior work suggests that the feedback helps LLMs ground their generations more effectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gehring et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Building on this, we integrated feedback into the process by explicitly indicating which generated constraints were incorrect or non-discriminative.
Thus, we propose the following two feedback-driven Multi-Turn approaches:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Greedy Multi-Turn. The first approach employed a simple feedback mechanism in which Llama 4 Scout </span><span id="S2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repeatedly attempted to produce a correct postcondition</span><span id="S2.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> until all test cases passed and a desired completeness is reached, using feedback from previous failed attempts. This feedback includes either binary pass/fail signals or syntax errors. The iterative process enabled the model to progressively cover the condition (1) in the first attempt, the condition (2) in the second, and the condition (3) in the third, eventually combining them into a single assertion by the fourth attempt. Within the 12-attempt limit, the model focused predominantly on these three conditions, but failed to generate the condition (4). The generated assertions consistently began with </span><span id="S2.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert return_value == -1 or ...</span><span id="S2.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, a pattern that could be trivially satisfied by faulty code returning <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> in all cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploratory Multi-turn. In real-world learning, students rarely solve complex problems correctly in a single attempt. Effective learners instead ask clarifying questions, explore partial solutions, and test their understanding before committing to a final answer. This differs from blindly retrying after a “try again” signal, and from single-pass solving without refinement. Inspired by this, we propose Exploratory Multi-turn for postcondition generation, where the LLM acts not as a code-to-code generator but as an active reasoner. It autonomously explores intermediate reasoning paths, probes partial answers, internalizes feedback, and decides when it has enough evidence to produce the result.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In the example, similar to Greedy Multi-turn, the first three attempts correctly generated conditions (1)–(3). In the 4th attempt, a minor change triggered an </span><span id="S2.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AssertionError</span><span id="S2.p12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. Unlike Greedy, which often stagnates on the same incomplete assertion, Exploratory Multi-Turn broke from its prior pattern and shifted direction in the fifth attempt. The model explicitly noted: </span><span id="S2.p12.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The assertion failed, which means our current postconditions might not be sufficient or accurate. Let’s revisit and refine them… We can use a different approach… Let’s iterate over…”</span><span id="S2.p12.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. This shift produced a postcondition that covered condition (4) and discarded the overly permissive pattern </span><span id="S2.p12.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span><span id="S2.p12.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span><span id="S2.p12.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">return_value</span><span id="S2.p12.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> == -1 or …. It finally generated the correct and complete postcondition, suggesting that Exploratory Multi-Turn helps escape local optima and improves completeness.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section"><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">3</span> </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Given a function </span><math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span><math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, where </span><math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and </span><math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> are the input and output domains, the goal is to synthesize a postcondition </span><math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{I}\times\mathcal{O}\rightarrow\{{\texttt{true}},{\texttt{false}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">true</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">false</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{I}\times\mathcal{O}\rightarrow\{{\texttt{true}},{\texttt{false}}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> that characterizes the correct behavior of </span><math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The postcondition </span><math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> is a logical predicate that must hold for any input-output pair </span><math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,o)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> where </span><math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=f(i)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. Formally: </span><math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i\in\mathcal{I},\quad\phi(i,f(i))=\texttt{true}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">true</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i\in\mathcal{I},\quad\phi(i,f(i))=\texttt{true}.</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
In practice, </span><math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> is often expressed as an assertion or a condition over program variables after execution. The goal is to infer such a </span><math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> automatically, using the following inputs: a function’s source code, a finite test suite </span><math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{(i_{1},o_{1}),\dots,(i_{n},o_{n})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{(i_{1},o_{1}),\dots,(i_{n},o_{n})\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, and a set of mutants </span><math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> of the original </span><math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. Two evaluation criteria for a synthesized postcondition </span><math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> include</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness (Corr.): The postcondition must hold on all known correct executions captured in the test suite: </span><math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall(i_{j},o_{j})\in\mathcal{T},\quad\phi(i_{j},o_{j})={\texttt{true}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">true</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall(i_{j},o_{j})\in\mathcal{T},\quad\phi(i_{j},o_{j})={\texttt{true}}.</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> The postcondition must reject incorrect variants.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness (Comp.): Given a set of program mutants </span><math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{f^{\prime}_{1},f^{\prime}_{2},\dots,f^{\prime}_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{f^{\prime}_{1},f^{\prime}_{2},\dots,f^{\prime}_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, the completeness of a postcondition measures the proportion of mutants for which the postcondition fails:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{Comp}(\phi)=\frac{\left|\{f^{\prime}_{k}\in\mathcal{M}\mid\exists i\in\mathcal{I},\ \phi(i,f^{\prime}_{k}(i))={\texttt{false}}\}\right|}{|\mathcal{M}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">false</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{Comp}(\phi)=\frac{\left|\{f^{\prime}_{k}\in\mathcal{M}\mid\exists i\in\mathcal{I},\ \phi(i,f^{\prime}_{k}(i))={\texttt{false}}\}\right|}{|\mathcal{M}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness ensures consistency with the given code, while completeness reflects the specification’s robustness in identifying deviations/defects.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective of Postcondition Inference/Generation.
Given a function </span><math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, a test suite </span><math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, and a set of mutants </span><math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, the objective of </span><span id="S3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> is to synthesize a postcondition </span><math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> that:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Achieves full correctness over </span><math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">;</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Maximizes completeness over </span><math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">;</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section"><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">4</span> </span>Exploratory Multi-turn Algorithm</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Algorithm supporting information</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Completeness Score <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: Each feedback evaluation returns a quantitative measure of completeness (e.g., how much of the expected behavior is covered).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Threshold <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: A predefined target completeness level. The loop continues until the model achieves this threshold or chooses to stop early.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Best-so-far Tracking</span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: The algorithm keeps track of the best postcondition submitted by the model so far (based on the highest completeness score) and returns it if the loop ends prematurely.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM.decidesToStop.</span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> At each attempt, the model may choose to submit a postcondition once it is confident after a process of exploration and refinement or reaches a maximum number of attempts. The submitted postcondition is then evaluated and, if it improves upon the </span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Best-so-far Tracking</span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and satisfies the completeness threshold, the algorithm triggers </span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM.decidesToStop</span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> to terminate and return the best postcondition generated so far.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:222.9pt;height:310.5pt;vertical-align:-152.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-5.9pt,8.2pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:231.3pt;">
<div id="S4.F2.fig1.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_listing">
<div id="algx1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                  
                  
                <span id="algx1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function code </span><math id="algx1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, test suite </span><math id="algx1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, completeness threshold </span><math id="algx1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                  
                  
                <span id="algx1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final postcondition </span><math id="algx1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">final</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                  
                  
                <math id="algx1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\leftarrow[\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\leftarrow[\,]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span><span id="algx1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> History buffer
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                  
                  
                <math id="algx1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\text{best}}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">best</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\text{best}}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, </span><math id="algx1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{best}}\leftarrow 0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">best</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{best}}\leftarrow 0.0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                  
                  
                <span id="algx1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while True do
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                  
                  
                <span id="algx1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">   </span><math id="algx1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="prompt\leftarrow\text{constructPrompt}(f,\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">constructPrompt</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">prompt\leftarrow\text{constructPrompt}(f,\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algx1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
