<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">We aim to answer the following questions:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1. [Effectiveness–Efficiency]. How effective and efficient is our tool in postcondition inference?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2. [Reasoning]. How does </span><span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> with Exploratory Multi-turn </span><span id="S6.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">perform reasoning</span><span id="S6.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3. [Stratifying Results]. How does </span><span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> with feedback perform on </span><span id="S6.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">hard cases</span><span id="S6.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4. [Cost Efficiency]. How efficient is </span><span id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> in token costs and actual costs?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks. We selected EvalPlus as our dataset as it was used in </span><span id="S6.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite><span id="S6.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. It has 164 Python problems, each with a function stub, textual description, reference implementation, and validation tests </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a></cite><span id="S6.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. EvalPlus updates the HumanEval benchmark </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite><span id="S6.p6.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, containing the same problems but with more extensive test suites (</span><math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p6.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> 775 test cases per problem). We also used FixEval </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anjum Haque et al. (2023)</a></cite><span id="S6.p6.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> for bug detection experiment (see Appendix </span><a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Bug Detection via Generated Postconditions ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a><span id="S6.p6.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Baselines. We chose as baseline the state-of-the-art </span><span id="S6.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, with access to the reference implementation. </span><span id="S6.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> was shown to outperform prior approaches such as TOGA </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinella et al. (2022)</a></cite><span id="S6.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and Daikon </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ernst et al. (1999)</a></cite><span id="S6.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We used Llama 4 Scout as the underlying LLM for both </span><span id="S6.p7.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.p7.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and our tool in all experiments.</span><span id="S6.p7.1.14" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics. We use two key metrics: 1) </span><span id="S6.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Correctness</span><span id="S6.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> refers to whether the postcondition passes all test cases in the given test suite; 2) Completeness is the percentage of mutants distinguishable (i.e., rejected) by the postcondition given the test suite. </span><span id="S6.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We used the same mutant set from <span id="S6.p8.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">nl2postcond</span> for a fair comparison.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">To calculate the efficiency over submission attempts, we define the efficiency score in a setting as </span><math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{\text{completeness score}_{i}}{\text{number of submission turns}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mfrac><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">completeness score</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">number of submission turns</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{\text{completeness score}_{i}}{\text{number of submission turns}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, where </span><math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> is the number of functions in the dataset. The efficiency score </span><math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> measures the average increase of completeness after one submission attempt.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.1</span> </span>Effectiveness and Efficiency (RQ1)</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The values in bold in Table </span><a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Empirical Evaluation ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> indicate </span><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the best results</span><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> across all configurations, with Exploratory achieving peak performance at </span><math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=90, </span><math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=12 (99.4% correctness, 89.6% completeness) while requiring an average of 7.2 attempts and 1.7 submissions per task. </span><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> achieves 73.3% correctness and 36.0% completeness using a single attempt.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Across most settings, for both Greedy and Exploratory Multi-turn, despite allowing a higher maximum number of attempts, </span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the average number of attempts range between 2.3-7.2, with the correctness increasing from 84.3% to 99.4% and completeness increasing from 69.2% to 89.6%</span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. The higher maximum number of attempts allowed, the higher correctness and completeness for Greedy and Exploratory Multi-turn. For Exploratory Multi-turn, among 3.8-7.2 attempts, it explores 3.5 times on average and submits the resulting postcondition from 1.0 to 1.7 times. As seen, on average, </span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> uses </span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a small number of attempts</span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and does not need to use up the attempt budget. However, the highest numbers of attempts reach the maximum budget. That means that </span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it needs to use all the budget of attempts in hard cases</span><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (see RQ3).</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.1.1</span> </span>Correctness</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">We first study the effect of the attempt budget </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.
With </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, both Exploratory and Greedy Multi-turn outperform </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. Under the most relaxed setting (</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=50, </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=4), Greedy reaches 93.7% correctness in 2.3 attempts on average, a 20-point gain over </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (73.3%). Exploratory also improves over </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, but achieves 84.3% due to limited exploratory turns.
Exploratory benefits more from larger </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">: the average number of functions with correct postconditions rises from 139.0 (</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">) to 153.7 (</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=8) and 161.3 (</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=12). Greedy improves less with </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. On average, increasing </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> by 4 yields a 6.8% correctness gain for Exploratory versus 2.1% for Greedy, as Exploratory can probe more candidates before submission (Section </span><a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 SpecMind’s Reasoning at Each Attempt ‣ 6 Empirical Evaluation ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">For comparison, we also evaluate Random Sampling with </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, i.e., querying the LLM independently </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> times with early stopping when </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> is met. Random Sampling achieves 79.2% correctness at (</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=50, </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=4) and peaks at 84.9% under the strictest setting (</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">). Under the same setting, Greedy and Exploratory reach 98.7% and 99.4% correctness, using 6.2 and 7.2 attempts on average, compared to 10.3 for Random Sampling.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.1.2</span> </span>Completeness</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploratory Multi-turn outperforms Greedy in all settings except </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90,\mu{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90,\mu{=}4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. The smallest completeness gap is at </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}50,\mu{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}50,\mu{=}4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (70.6% vs. 69.2%), and the largest at </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}50,\mu{=}12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}50,\mu{=}12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (86.4% vs. 75.0%). Our tool’s lowest completeness is 68.6% at </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}70,\mu{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}70,\mu{=}4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, far above single-pass </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (36.0%), highlighting the benefit of multi-turn refinement for more discriminative specifications. Random Sampling improves with more attempts (e.g., at </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, 41.5% with </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu{=}4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> to 52.6% with </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu{=}12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu{=}12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">) but remains well below </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">’s worst case (68.6%). </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We also analyze the dynamics of completeness scores across submission attempts.
Representative trends and aggregate statistics are in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Behavioral Patterns in Completeness Across Submissions ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a></span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></p>
</div>
