</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.1.3</span> </span>Efficiency</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fig. </span><a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.1.2 Completeness ‣ 6.1 Effectiveness and Efficiency (RQ1) ‣ 6 Empirical Evaluation ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> shows that Exploratory Multi-turn is more submission-efficient than Greedy, peaking at 80.0% vs. 47.7%. That is, the average completeness gain per submission is 1.67</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> higher for Exploratory. Raising the completeness threshold increases final completeness but reduces efficiency: from </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}50</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> to </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, efficiency drops by 5.7% for Exploratory and 14.5% for Greedy, suggesting that more discriminative postconditions require more exploratories.
Compared to Random Sampling, Exploratory is more efficient (80.0% vs. 26.3%) and degrades less as </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> increases. As </span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> rises from 50-70-90, Exploratory achieves 3.04</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, 4.17</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, and 5.59</span><math id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> the efficiency of Random Sampling. </span><span id="S6.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">See a case study comparing two approaches in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 A Case Study of Exploratory vs. Greedy Multi-turn ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.
</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.2</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span>’s Reasoning at Each Attempt</h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.2.1</span> </span>Experimental Methodology</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This experiment analyzes </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">’s reasoning behavior across attempts, focusing on Exploratory Multi-turn because each attempt may depend on prior exploratory or submission turns. (Greedy Multi-turn contains only submission turns, which are a subset of Exploratory.) We use </span><math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}70</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> since </span><math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}50</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> often stops too early and </span><math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> frequently exhausts the budget without meeting the threshold. We set </span><math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu{=}12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu{=}12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> to observe behavior over more attempts. We analyze all natural-language reasoning texts produced per attempt (enclosed by </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">…</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/think&gt;</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">), totaling 1,026 instances; each case has 6.5 reasoning instances on average.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">To categorize reasoning behaviors, we first use an LLM to cluster reasoning texts in five batches (200–226 each), producing distinct, non-overlapping categories. We then manually consolidate and refine these into seven observable categories. Finally, we use an LLM to classify all reasoning texts into the seven categories for consistent labeling, and manually validate the results by inspecting a random 10% sample per category.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.2.2</span> </span>Reasoning of Exploratory Multi-turn</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Table </span><a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1.2 Completeness ‣ 6.1 Effectiveness and Efficiency (RQ1) ‣ 6 Empirical Evaluation ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> presents the categories of </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">’s reasoning along with their descriptions, frequencies, and representative examples. The most frequent behavior is </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Refine Assertions</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, appearing in 419 reasonings, where the model improves existing assertions to make them more concise and comprehensive. Typical phrases include: </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“To refine our postconditions…”</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“A more accurate property might be…”</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, or </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The current postcondition does not accurately reflect…”</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. The second most frequent category is </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Verify Function Behavior</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, with 270 reasonings. In this category, the model proposes assertions to verify functional properties.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/return_value_type_and_range.png" id="S6.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/311;" width="476" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(a)</span> </span>Ret. Val. Type/Range</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/check_base_cases.png" id="S6.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/315;" width="476" height="315" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(b)</span> </span>Check Base Cases</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/check_edge_cases.png" id="S6.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/311;" width="476" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(c)</span> </span>Check Edge Cases</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/postcondition_combination.png" id="S6.F5.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/311;" width="476" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(d)</span> </span>Postcond. Comb.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/refine_assertions.png" id="S6.F5.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/311;" width="476" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(e)</span> </span>Refine Assertions</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/verify_function_behavior.png" id="S6.F5.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/311;" width="476" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(f)</span> </span>Verify Function Behavior</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/RQ2/submit_final_solution.png" id="S6.F5.sf7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/311;" width="476" height="311" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.sf7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(g)</span> </span>Submit A Solution</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Figure 5</span>: </span>Frequency distribution analysis from seven reasoning categories across attempts (RQ2)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning Patterns. Fig. </span><a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.2.2 Reasoning of Exploratory Multi-turn ‣ 6.2 SpecMind’s Reasoning at Each Attempt ‣ 6 Empirical Evaluation ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> shows the occurrences of </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">7 categories across attempts</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> for all samples, excluding unsolvable cases.
</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Return Value Type and Range</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Verify Function Behavior</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> concentrate in the first attempt (49 and 94 instances). </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Check Base Cases</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Check Edge Cases</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> also appear early, peaking at the 2nd (8) and 3rd (17) attempts, indicating that the model initially explores return values and base/edge cases. </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Submit Final Solution</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> occurs from attempts 3–12 and peaks around attempts 5–6, consistent with Exploratory requiring about 5–6 attempts per case. </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Refine Assertions</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> is frequent between attempts 2–6, peaking at attempts 3–4 (74 and 68), suggesting the model refines before submitting. </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Postcondition Combination</span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> emerges at attempt 3 and peaks at attempt 5 (14), indicating it combines exploratory results before submitting. </span><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Example Trajectory of Reasoning Categories ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> for an example.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.3</span> </span>Stratifying Results on Hard Cases (RQ3)</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">We analyze the hardest cases: the model exhausts the attempt budget without reaching the completeness threshold, under the strict setting </span><math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90,\mu{=}12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90,\mu{=}12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. This yields 23 hard cases out of 159: one with no correct postcondition and 22 with completeness </span><math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;90</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (average 56.2%).
In the standard setting, we provide only binary feedback: whether a postcondition passes all tests (correctness) and whether it catches all mutants (completeness). For these hard cases, we add richer feedback by returning a randomly-selected uncaught mutant and asking the model for refinement. We then extend the budget by 4 attempts (</span><math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu{=}16</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">) to measure the impact.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">With enhanced feedback, correct postconditions increase to 22/23 and average completeness rises to 85.1%; the model reaches the threshold in 14/22 correct cases. Applying binary feedback first (</span><math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90,\mu{=}12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90,\mu{=}12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">) and using enhanced feedback only for the remaining hard cases with 4 extra attempts improves performance to 99.7% correctness and 92.0% completeness (vs. 99.4% and 89.6% with binary feedback only).
If we use enhanced feedback from the start with </span><math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}90,\mu{=}12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}90,\mu{=}12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, the model produces correct postconditions for 21/23 hard cases; among 22 correct cases, 9 reach the completeness target, with overall average completeness 90.6%.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6.4</span> </span>LLM Token Usage &amp; Cost Analysis (RQ4)</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">We analyze the token usage between Random Sampling (running </span><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span><math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> independent times) and </span><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecMind</span><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. Under the strictest configuration (completeness threshold </span><math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=90, maximum number of turns </span><math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=12), Random Sampling uses an average of 302 tokens per instance (min=19 and max=1,666 tokens), which is much lower than the usages of Multi-turn approaches.
Greedy uses an average of 5,752 tokens per instance (ranging from 266 to 25,909), while Exploratory uses an average of 8,299 tokens per instance (ranging 1,313–29,956).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The higher token usage per instance for our tool stems from two factors: (1) inclusion of Chain-of-Thought tokens (</span><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> does not have CoT) and (2) inclusion of the history buffer (Equation </span><a href="#S5.Ex2" title="5 Prompt Design and Feedback ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">). However, the actual costs remain modest: 0.18 cent and 0.25 cent per instance for Greedy and Exploratory, respectively (</span><math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=90, </span><math id="S6.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=12).</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20610v1/figures/token_bug_tradeoff_upd.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/147;" width="191" height="147" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Figure 6</span>: </span>Token Usages <math id="S6.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\&amp;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&amp;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\&amp;</annotation></semantics></math> Numbers of Caught Mutants</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This high token usage is a tradeoff for more bug detection. Fig. </span><a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.4 LLM Token Usage &amp; Cost Analysis (RQ4) ‣ 6 Empirical Evaluation ‣ SpecMind: Cognitively Inspired, Interactive Multi-Turn Framework for Postcondition Inference" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> displays the trends for Random Sampling, Greedy, and Exploratory when the completeness threshold increases (</span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=50,70,90). When </span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> increases, each approach uses more tokens due to more attempts. However, across thresholds, both Greedy and Exploratory catch more mutants/bugs than Random Sampling. Greedy caught 24.7 mutants with 3,065.9 tokens per instance (</span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> 0.0029 cent/bug) and 27.8 mutants with 5,752 tokens per instance (</span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> 0.0047 cent/bug). Exploratory achieved the highest bug detection performance, reaching 29.2 mutants with 6110.5 tokens per instance (</span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> 0.0046 cent/bug) and peaking at 30.9 with 8,299 tokens per instances (</span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> 0.0058 cent/bug). Random Sampling caught 17.6 mutants with 298 tokens. Using more tokens (3,182 tokens) with maximum allowed attempts </span><math id="S6.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">=170, its number of detected bugs reaches to 23.3 (1.4 bugs less compared to 24.7 bugs by Greedy with 3k tokens), with 0.0082 cent/bug (2.83X cost more). Random Sampling has higher cost as it generates more output tokens (which cost more), whereas our tool requires more input tokens but produces fewer outputs. In brief, with lower attempts, Exploratory incurs lower costs with more detected bugs.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">Limitations &amp; Threats to Validity</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our evaluation is conducted on EvalPlus </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite><span id="Sx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, which may not reflect the full diversity of real-world codebases. While EvalPlus is a large public benchmark with postcondition annotations and enables a fair comparison against </span><span id="Sx1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nl2postcond</span><span id="Sx1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> on its original setting, it primarily consists of Python tasks of moderate complexity. Thus, generalization to larger systems, other programming languages, or other forms of specifications is not guaranteed. In addition, we evaluate mainly GPT-family models; performance may differ for other model families or future versions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our results may also be influenced by the underlying pre-training knowledge of LLMs. Although we cannot fully rule out exposure to similar code during pretraining, our evaluation centers on generating new postconditions and validating them via execution, which is unlikely to be explained solely by memorization.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">We operationalize postcondition quality using two proxies: correctness (passing the test suite) and completeness (rejecting mutants). Both measures inherit limitations from their backends: correctness is bounded by test coverage, and completeness depends on the quality and representativeness of the mutant set. If the tests or mutants fail to capture realistic behaviors or defects, our metrics may overestimate true specification quality or bug-detection usefulness.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finally, our conclusions depend on experimental choices such as the attempt budget </span><math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and completeness threshold </span><math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. While we evaluate across multiple configurations to reduce sensitivity to any single setting, other datasets, feedback designs, or hyperparameter choices could change the observed trade-offs between effectiveness and efficiency.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
