<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix"><span id="A1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Appendix A</span> </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="A1.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">A.1</span> </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Researchers have proposed automated approaches that fall into three categories: </span><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">program analysis-based</span><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, </span><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">data mining-based</span><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, and </span><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">large language model (LLM)-based</span><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Program analysis-based methods use dynamic or static techniques. Dynamic approaches </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ammons et al. (2002)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beschastnikh et al. (2011)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ernst et al. (1999)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> infer properties by monitoring executions but depend heavily on test coverage. Static analyses </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shoham et al. (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engler et al. (2001)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kremenek et al. (2006)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramanathan et al. (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2011)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> and abstract interpretation </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cousot et al. (2012)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> avoid this dependency but often produce conservative or imprecise specifications due to high false-positive rates. Several approaches attempted to generate specification by analyzing API documentation or code comments </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pandita et al. (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. (2011)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blasi et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2009b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2017)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data mining-based approaches extract common API usage patterns from large codebases </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Zhou (2005)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Livshits and Zimmermann (2005)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2009a)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. They identify call pairs, sequences, and finite-state models </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wasylkowski et al. (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams and Hollingsworth (2005)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thummalapenta and Xie (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradel and Gross (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wasylkowski and Zeller (2009)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. While useful for API usages, they rarely infer semantic specifications such as pre/postconditions </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramanathan et al. (2007)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In general, ML approaches for specification generation have advanced in multiple directions, including postcondition inference </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Endres et al. (2024)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, synthesizing test oracles </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinella et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mastropaolo et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tufano et al. (2022)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, improving test coverage </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lemieux et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Altmayer Pizzorno and Berger (2025)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, and generating unit tests </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lahiri et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tufano et al. (2021)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. These techniques rely on different types of inputs. AthenaTest </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tufano et al. (2021)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> generates both the input and the oracle of a unit test directly from the implementation of the focal method, while TOGA generates only the oracle </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinella et al. (2022)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">. TiCoder </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lahiri et al. (2023)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> leverages LLMs to produce both inputs and outputs based on a natural language description of user intent.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">While these methods target the production of concrete test cases (and sometimes oracles), our work instead focuses on generating postconditions. Similarly, EvoSpex </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Molina et al. (2021)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> applies evolutionary learning to infer functional input–output relationships.
More recent efforts have explored property-based specifications. Vikram </span><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vikram et al. (2024)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> propose leveraging LLMs to generate property-based tests (PBTs), while Speculyzer</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Key et al. (2022)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> enumerates likely properties and candidate inputs with LLMs, but uses them primarily as heuristics to guide code generation rather than to ensure correctness or completeness of specifications. Beyond input–output properties, machine learning has also been applied to infer intermediate specifications, such as program invariants </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laich et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pei et al. (2023)</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Table 3</span>: </span>Performance on Distinguishing Bugs. <math id="A1.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>: # of submissions; R.Sampl.: run <span id="A1.T3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">nl2postcond</span> <math id="A1.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> independent times; Corr.: correctness; Dist. Bugs: # of distinguishable bugs.</figcaption>
